      "writtenOn": "2018-06-29T07:38:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e\u003e   * 1) pointer-to-next, sizeof(uint32_t): just return zeroes, we are not part of a chain.\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e We shouldn\u0027t ever be called with this offset, so I ignored this for now - do you think I should be handling this? My thinking was that it would be better to double check that it won\u0027t ever happen, and then assert that it doesn\u0027t (or similar)\n\u003e \n\u003e Agreed, it shouldn\u0027t happen. There are two ways to handle: assert() or log, return 0 and continue. We can assert() but we need to make sure that  it\u0027s a full-blown cr50 reboot that causes whole system (host) reboot then; just restarting cr50 w/o the rest of the system is a security issue. If we can\u0027t guarantee that, let\u0027s use the 2nd option. \n\u003e As for logging stuff, we may want to use this vNVRAM functionality for reporting counters that then can be picked by UMA. But too early to think about that.\n\u003e \n\nI checked on my eve, and a failing assertion does cause a full system reboot. The output from the CCD console was:\n\n--- UART initialized after reboot ---                                                          \n[Reset cause: hard]      \n..(followed by initialization)\n\nLet me know if you want me to do more verification of this\n\n\u003e \u003e\u003e   * 2) header, sizeof(NV_INDEX): set the index number in the common template and copy from there - \n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e Do you mean change the value in the template before copying? The reason I did it like this was so that I could have it be const, which I was told would mean it ended up getting stored in flash rather than ram, which seemed like a Good Thing given this will presumably be called very infrequently. What do you think?\n\u003e \n\u003e Yes, sorry, over-simplified things in my writing. having it const, and substituting the index on the fly is also good.  Though nowadays flash is as precious resource as RAM is in cr50 :)\n\u003e \n\nright.. just saw your comment on a related bug ;)\n\nI have updated the code so that it is possible to read both the NV_INDEX and actual data together, which I think was something you suggested. I haven\u0027t done it as an actual loop as it would only have at most two iterations and it felt a little strange. I also haven\u0027t allowed reading only part of NV_INDEX - comments in the code; let me know what you think about this. I don\u0027t feel particularly happy with how this is now, but I think it\u0027s better than before (which I realized had a bug in it anyway, not supporting user-defined offsets into the data - facepalm). \n\n\u003e \u003e\u003e   * 3) the actual data: invoke an index-specific callback?\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e Sounds good, I wasn\u0027t sure what the standard way to hook this up [in C] was though - do you have any preferences?\n\u003e \n\u003e Let\u0027s just go with whatever is already used for similar purposes in cr50. E.g. we have DECLARE_EXTENSION_COMMAND and DECLARE_VENDOR_COMMAND for supporting lists of command handlers. Can do something similar. And NvIndexToNvOffset can first just walk this list to check that the index is present, in case index numbers are not consecutive (e.g. we delete an obsolete vNVindex later).\n\nThanks - I had a look at those, I think it can work but is perhaps a little heavy for our needs? Before going ahead, wanted to check if you had a preference for re-using eg DECLARE_EXTENSION_COMMAND, or whether implementing a perhaps lighter weight but similar thing just for this use case is worth considering?",
